  "text": "Fourteen down. Four more, quickly. To aggregate a stream without holding rows, fold each record into a running dict as it arrives. Memory grows with the groups, not the rows: here, two. Reach for pandas when the data fits in memory and you want vectorized columns, group by and joins. Stay with generators when it streams, or won't fit. Group by splits the rows by key, sums each group, and combines the answers. As index equals false keeps the region as a column: EU two twenty-five, US one fifty. And the common pitfalls: membership tests against a list, strings built with plus in a loop, iter rows where a column operation exists, a data frame grown concat by concat, and a whole file read that could stream. Put these together, run them every night on a schedule, and one script has become a pipeline. Next: pipelines and systems.",
